{"text": " A radio broadcast carried Portuguese for your ears and Japanese for a screen. A 1988 article describes a July 10th broadcast on Brazil's R\u00e1dio USP. It used the two channels of stereo for different jobs. One carried the presenter's Portuguese speech. The other carried encoded sound representing the same text in Japanese. A computer connected to the radio could decode the signal and display the text. The Japanese text was already in the transmitted data. The computer's job was to read it. So, one broadcast offered two ways to receive the message. Listen in Portuguese or read in Japanese.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.98, "text": " A radio broadcast carried Portuguese for your ears and Japanese for a screen.", "tokens": [50365, 316, 6477, 9975, 9094, 22759, 337, 428, 8798, 293, 5433, 337, 257, 2568, 13, 50616], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15326577044547873, "compression_ratio": 1.576470588235294, "no_speech_prob": 6.844609948264413e-12, "words": [{"word": " A", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.18, "probability": 0.884135365486145}, {"word": " radio", "start": 0.18, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9897928237915039}, {"word": " broadcast", "start": 0.48, "end": 1.0, "probability": 0.998624324798584}, {"word": " carried", "start": 1.0, "end": 1.6, "probability": 0.9976627826690674}, {"word": " Portuguese", "start": 1.6, "end": 2.1, "probability": 0.9850900173187256}, {"word": " for", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.5, "probability": 0.9960260391235352}, {"word": " your", "start": 2.5, "end": 2.7, "probability": 0.9933909177780151}, {"word": " ears", "start": 2.7, "end": 3.22, "probability": 0.9986253976821899}, {"word": " and", "start": 3.22, "end": 3.6, "probability": 0.9669052362442017}, {"word": " Japanese", "start": 3.6, "end": 4.04, "probability": 0.9989086389541626}, {"word": " for", "start": 4.04, "end": 4.48, "probability": 0.9993541836738586}, {"word": " a", "start": 4.48, "end": 4.62, "probability": 0.9989416003227234}, {"word":